  "image_name": "73d6f148ffe9127c68d3340326d2432d.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several ways hydrogen can be activated and transferred on a supported metal particle. A pink nanoparticle sits on a blue support, and the four cartoons compare different reaction pathways at this interface.\n\nThe first two panels contrast two fundamental modes of H2 splitting. In homolytic dissociation, the H–H bond breaks evenly on the metal surface, giving two neutral H atoms adsorbed on the particle. In heterolytic dissociation, the bond breaks unevenly across the metal–support boundary, producing a hydride-like H− on the metal and a proton-like H+ on the support.\n\nA third pathway shows H2 activation assisted by an adsorbed ligand on the metal. Here, the ligand appears to participate in the polarization of hydrogen, again leading to separated negative and positive hydrogen species.\n\nThe final panel illustrates hydrogen transfer from the activated surface to an organic molecule. Dashed lines and highlighted red H atoms suggest delivery of hydrogen from the catalyst to a ring-containing substrate, forming a more hydrogenated product and then a further transformed product. Overall, the image emphasizes that hydrogen activation can proceed by multiple mechanisms, and that the metal surface, support, and ligands together control how hydrogen is generated and transferred in catalysis.",
